Step 1, sequential. Use a client **with a timeout** (the default one has none), a request with `ctx`, and always close the body.

```go
var client = &http.Client{Timeout: 5 * time.Second}

func check(ctx context.Context, url string) bool {
	req, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, http.MethodGet, url, nil)
	if err != nil {
		return false
	}
	resp, err := client.Do(req)
	if err != nil {
		return false
	}
	defer resp.Body.Close()
	return resp.StatusCode == http.StatusOK
}

func Sequential(ctx context.Context, urls []string) {
	for _, u := range urls {
		if check(ctx, u) {
			fmt.Println(u, "ok")
		} else {
			fmt.Println(u, "not ok")
		}
	}
}
```

Steps 2-4: URLs from a channel, a bounded worker pool, and one printer goroutine. This covers the slice case too (feed the slice into a channel).

```go
func CheckStream(ctx context.Context, urls <-chan string, workers int) {
	results := make(chan string)
	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	for range workers {
		wg.Go(func() {
			for u := range urls {
				status := "not ok"
				if check(ctx, u) {
					status = "ok"
				}
				select {
				case results <- u + " " + status:
				case <-ctx.Done():
					return
				}
			}
		})
	}
	go func() {
		wg.Wait()
		close(results)
	}()

	printed := make(chan struct{})
	go func() {
		defer close(printed)
		for r := range results {
			fmt.Println(r)
		}
	}()
	<-printed
}
```

Key points: only the sender side closes `results`, after all workers finish; `main` waits for the printer, or the last lines are lost when `main` returns; output order is arbitrary (carry an index if order matters). Invalid URLs like `http://ёёёё` just produce an error, so "not ok".

Unbuffered vs buffered: unbuffered is a synchronous hand-off. It gives backpressure (workers can't run ahead of the printer) and makes goroutine lifetimes obvious. A buffer only absorbs bursts; it doesn't fix logic bugs, and it can hide leaks and deadlocks until the buffer fills. A buffer of `len(urls)` is fine when the count is known and you want workers to never block on the consumer.

Review of the channel-based draft shown in one interview: `processURLs` drains the whole input before returning (not streaming), starts one goroutine per URL (unbounded), and uses `http.Get` without a timeout.

